    "c1": {
      "anchor": "t41",
      "kind": "item",
      "label": "Follow up: P26: a library's own new thing is made by name only for the types its project file exports",
      "excerpt": "Follow up: P26: a library's own new thing is made by name only for the types its project file exports",
      "text": "clarify",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-07T22:12:14.764Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-07T22:13:39.767Z",
          "html": "<p>What T41 means, in plain words:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>\n    A library (say, a cards library) can make things of its own kinds: its deck makes cards with <code>a new card</code>.\n  </li>\n  <li>\n    When Solitaire uses that library, you'd want the deck to make <em>Solitaire's</em> card (with Solitaire's own looks and\n    rules), not the library's plain one.\n  </li>\n  <li>\n    Since P26 that happens, but only when the library's project file lists the type in its <code>exports</code>:\n    <code>\"exports\": [\"Card\", \"Deck\"]</code>. Without that line, the deck keeps making the library's own plain cards.\n  </li>\n  <li>\n    I chose that so no existing project's compiled code changed. Making it the rule for every type would have changed 5\n    files in recipe-box, recetario, live-room and sala-en-vivo, for no gain.\n  </li>\n</ul>\n<p>The follow-up question T41 holds: is \"only what <code>exports</code> lists\" the rule you want, or should a library always let the project using it supply its own version of each type?</p>\n<epic-net-effect><ul><li>Nothing changes unless you pick the other rule;  then every library type is made by name, and those 5 files' compiled code changes.</li></ul></epic-net-effect>"
        }
      ]
    },
    "c2": {
      "anchor": "i83",
      "kind": "item",
      "label": "Rename from a `for` loop's variable where it's declared (`for card in the deck`, on `card`) gives nothing; from a use of it (`the card`) it works. Found as the Deck's rename test moved off `the rank`,",
      "excerpt": "Rename from a `for` loop's variable where it's declared (`for card in the deck`, on `card`) gives nothing; from a use of it (`the card`) it works. Found as the Deck's rename test moved off `the rank`,",
      "text": "wtf does this mean?",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-14T08:49:39.758Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-14T08:50:18.667Z",
          "html": "<p>Sorry, that was written in code-speak.  In plain words:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>In the editor, <b>Rename</b> (F2) changes a name everywhere it's used.</li>\n  <li>In Deck.spell, put the cursor on <code>card</code> in <code>for card in the deck</code>, the line that MAKES the name, and Rename:  nothing happens.</li>\n  <li>Put it on <code>card</code> further down, in <code>the short name of the card</code>, and Rename works:  every <code>card</code> changes.</li>\n  <li>So renaming only works from a place the name is used, not from the line that makes it.  Small, but surprising.</li>\n  <li>I found it because a test renamed <code>rank</code> in Deck.spell, and your <code>with rank and suit</code> took that use of <code>rank</code> away.</li>\n</ul>\n<epic-net-effect><ul><li>Nothing changes until it's fixed;  it's a small editor bug, not urgent.  Mark it Do this if you want it fixed now.</li></ul></epic-net-effect>"
        }
      ],
      "claim": {
        "by": "owenoak",
        "agent": "claude",
        "at": "2026-11-14T08:50:17.887Z"
      }
    },
    "c3": {
      "anchor": "i83",
      "kind": "item",
      "label": "Rename from a `for` loop's variable where it's declared (`for card in the deck`, on `card`) gives nothing; from a use of it (`the card`) it works. Found as the Deck's rename test moved off `the rank`,",
      "excerpt": "Rename from a `for` loop's variable where it's declared (`for card in the deck`, on `card`) gives nothing; from a use of it (`the card`) it works. Found as the Deck's rename test moved off `the rank`,",
      "text": "So this is fixed?  I asked for clarification",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-14T09:02:42.257Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-14T09:03:41.630Z",
          "html": "<p><b>No, it isn't fixed:  I83 is still open.</b>  My reply above was only the clarification you asked for, not a fix.  Short version:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>The editor's Rename (F2) does nothing when the cursor is on a loop's name where it's made:  <code>card</code> in <code>for card in the deck</code>.</li>\n  <li>From any later use of it (<code>the card</code>), Rename works.</li>\n</ul>\n<epic-net-effect><ul><li>Still open.  Mark it \"Do this\" and I'll fix it;  otherwise it waits.</li></ul></epic-net-effect>"
        }
      ],
      "claim": {
        "by": "owenoak",
        "agent": "claude",
        "at": "2026-11-14T09:03:41.056Z"
      }
    },
    "c4": {
      "anchor": "i83",
      "kind": "item",
      "label": "Rename from a `for` loop's variable where it's declared (`for card in the deck`, on `card`) gives nothing; from a use of it (`the card`) it works. Found as the Deck's rename test moved off `the rank`,",
      "excerpt": "Rename from a `for` loop's variable where it's declared (`for card in the deck`, on `card`) gives nothing; from a use of it (`the card`) it works. Found as the Deck's rename test moved off `the rank`,",
      "text": "What does \"rename form a `for` loop variable mean?",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-14T09:04:56.663Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-14T09:05:26.701Z",
          "html": "<p>Sorry, here it is with an example.  It's about the editor's <b>Rename</b> command:  right-click a word › <b>Rename Symbol</b> (or press F2), type a new name, and the editor changes that name everywhere it's used.</p>\n<p>In Deck.spell:</p>\n<pre>for card in the deck                          ← line A:  this line MAKES the name \"card\"\n\tadd the short name of the card to card names   ← line B:  this line USES it</pre>\n<ul>\n  <li>Click on <code>card</code> in line B, Rename it to <code>c</code>:  works, both lines change.</li>\n  <li>Click on <code>card</code> in line A, Rename it to <code>c</code>:  <b>nothing happens</b>.  That's the bug.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>\"A <code>for</code> loop's variable\" is just the name a <code>for</code> line makes, <code>card</code> here.</p>\n<epic-net-effect><ul><li>Still open, not fixed.  Mark it \"Do this\" if you want it fixed.</li></ul></epic-net-effect>"
        }
      ],
      "claim": {
        "by": "owenoak",
        "agent": "claude",
        "at": "2026-11-14T09:05:25.614Z"
      }
    }
  },
